To add an image to a web page using HTML, you can follow these steps to create the required HTML syntax:

1. Add an `<img>` tag to your HTML document. This tag represents an image on the webpage.

2. To specify the image source, add a source attribute (`src`) inside the `<img>` tag, and set its value to the URL or file path of the image you want to display. For example:

   - If the image is located at the URL `https://example.com/image.jpg`, the HTML syntax would be:

     ```html
     <img src="https://example.com/image.jpg">
     ```

   - If the image is located in a subdirectory named `images` at the same domain, the file path would be `/images/image.jpg`. The HTML syntax would be:

     ```html
     <img src="/images/image.jpg">
     ```

   - If the image is located on a different domain than the webpage, make sure you have permission to use the image or acquire the necessary permissions if the image is copyrighted.

3. (Optional) Add an alternative text (alt text) for the image using the `alt` attribute, inside the `<img>` tag. This is useful for accessibility purposes, as screen readers for visually impaired users will read the alternative text out loud to the user. For example:

   ```html
   <img src="https://example.com/image.jpg" alt="Description of the image">
   ```

4. (Optional) If you want to adjust the size of the image, you can specify its width and height values using the `width` and `height` attributes respectively, inside the `<img>` tag. For example:

   ```html
   <img src="https://example.com/image.jpg" width="200" height="200">
   ```

   Note: If the image has fixed size information, it's best to include the width and height values as numeric values, rather than using CSS values.

5. (Optional) If you want to control the alignment of the image on the page, you can use CSS to style an `<img>` element. Here are some common examples:

   - To align an image to the left, add a class to your `<img>` tag and reference a CSS style for it:

     ```html
     <img src="https://example.com/image.jpg" class="img-left">
     <!-- CSS style for the "img-left" class -->
     <style>
       .img-left {
         float: left;
         clear: none;
         max-width: 100%;
       }
     </style>
     ```

   - To align an image in the middle of the page horizontally, you can use the following CSS:

     ```html
     <img src="https://example.com/image.jpg" class="centered-img">
     <!-- CSS style for the "centered-img" class -->
     <style>
       .centered-img {
         max-width: 100%;
         display: block;
         margin: 0 auto;
         clear: none;
       }
     </style>
     ```

   - To align an image to the right, use the following CSS:

     ```html
     <img src="https://example.com/image.jpg" class="img-right">
     <!-- CSS style for the "img-right" class -->
     <style>
       .img-right {
         float: right;
         clear: none;
         max-width: 100%;
       }
     </style>
     ```

   - To align an image centrally within a parent element, use the following CSS:

     Suppose you have a wrapper element with a class `image-container`, and you want to align the image present inside it in the center horizontally. You can use the following CSS:

     ```html
     <div class="image-container">
       <img src="https://example.com/image.jpg" srcset="/images/image.jpg">
       <!-- CSS style for the "image-container" class -->
       <style>
         .image-container {
           max-width: 100%;
           text-align: center;
         }
         img {
           max-width: 100%;
           display: block;
           margin: 0;
           padding: 0;
         }
       </style>
     </div>
     ```

     In this example, the image will be centered horizontally within the element with the `image-container` class.

   Note: In the HTML code snippet above, I've also included the `srcset` attribute as an example of using the `srcset` attribute which allows you to specify multiple images sources for different device resolutions or DPR (Device Pixel Ratio) by providing a list of images with their width in terms of the viewports they would be displayed on. However, this is an optional attribute and not required for adding an image to a page.

The HTML code snippets above are just examples, and you can customize them based on your specific use case. The required HTML syntax for adding an image to a page is the inclusion of an `<img>` tag with a valid source, and optional attributes like `alt`, `width`, `height`, and `class`. The optional styling using CSS will depend on your design requirements.

